+# Decision: Concurrency policy for independently edited visits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same visit independently — one worker fixes an observer name while another, comparing notes, revises a species identification and its cover estimate. Both edits start from the same fetched state and can arrive days apart. The record store must turn these into a current record without treating arrival order as authority and without losing either submission.
+
+The decision is which unit the server reconciles: whole visits, or individual fields. It sizes the rest of this node's public contract — submission envelope, retrieval shape, and resolution operation — and the tablet-sync and review-export nodes are built against whatever it produces. The invariant that no submitted edit is silently discarded holds under either option and is background, not a deciding element.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Scientific-relationship safety** (major — turns on the non-goal *no automated inference of scientific intent* and the tenet *do not silently discard a submitted edit*). A species identification and its cover estimate are one scientific judgment; observation date and observer list are bookkeeping. The server cannot tell a related pair from unrelated edits by field name. The question is whether the policy can ever combine two workers' values into one record that neither worker authored and no coordinator inspected.
+
+**Coordinator review workload** (moderate). Two coordinators clear the queue before a weekly Friday export against ~6,000 visits a season. Every visit the policy sends to review is coordinator time; every visit it readies automatically is deadline headroom. This is the criterion that most favors field-level merging.
+
+**Implementation and maintenance burden** (moderate — turns on the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*). Reconciliation logic has to stay correct across releases for scalar fields, observer lists, and species-entry collections, maintained by a team without a dedicated systems administrator.
+
+**Export inspectability** (moderate — turns on the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). A reviewer asking why an exported value reads as it does must be able to trace it back to submitted observations.
+
+**Submission and tablet-storage size** (minor). What each tablet must retain and send per edit.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission names the base revision it was edited from and carries the full visit payload. Two edits from the same base produce competing complete revisions — whole snapshots, each authored end to end by one worker. A coordinator resolves by selecting one competing revision as the visit's current revision; the others are retained as superseded history. The server never composes a record out of parts of two submissions.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A (base `r1`) submits a revision correcting the observer name; worker B (base `r1`) submits a revision adding a species. Both become competing heads; the visit is unresolved. A coordinator compares the two whole revisions and selects one. If the team wants both corrections in the record, a coordinator enters a revision carrying both — a human act, inspected, never an automatic field join.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific-relationship safety: **strong** — the server never combines field values, so it cannot join a species ID to a cover estimate the submitting worker did not pair.
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak** — any divergence of base, including edits to genuinely unrelated fields, produces a competing revision that a coordinator must resolve.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — reconciliation is revision selection; there are no per-field or per-collection merge rules to maintain.
+- Export inspectability: **strong** — each current revision is one submitted (or one coordinator-authored) whole payload; the exported value traces to a single author.
+- Submission and tablet-storage size: **strong** — a tablet retains a base revision ID and sends the payload it already holds.
+
+**Summary.** Trades coordinator review volume for the guarantee that no record is a machine-combined mix of two workers' observations.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies its base state and the specific field changes it makes. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a single current record; only changes that overlap on the same field are sent to review. The server maintains merge rules for each field kind — scalars (date, notes), the observer list, and the species-cover collection — deciding what "disjoint" and "overlapping" mean for each.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A changes the observer name; worker B adds a species. The fields are disjoint, so the server produces one record holding both changes with no review. Only if both workers edited the *same* species entry would the visit go to review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific-relationship safety: **weak** — "disjoint fields" is a structural test; a species identification and its cover estimate are different fields, so edits a scientist would treat as one judgment can be auto-combined across two submissions without anyone noticing. Requiring manual approval of every combined record would recover the safety but discard the workload advantage that is this option's reason to exist.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong** — unrelated edits combine automatically, so fewer visits reach the queue and more are export-ready by Friday.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — the merge rules for scalars, observer lists, and species collections must be authored and kept correct across releases by a team without a dedicated systems administrator.
+- Export inspectability: **partial** — achievable, but it requires field-level provenance (which submission each exported field came from) rather than a single authoring revision.
+- Submission and tablet-storage size: **partial** — a tablet must retain a base snapshot and compute and send structured per-field changes.
+
+**Summary.** Trades merge-rule machinery and the risk of silent scientific combination for a materially smaller review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.** The decision turns on scientific-relationship safety (major) against coordinator review workload (moderate). Field-aware merge wins the workload criterion outright — with two coordinators clearing a seasonal queue before a weekly deadline, that advantage is real, and it is why the option stays worth understanding rather than dismissing. It loses the major criterion: its automatic combination is a structural disjoint-field test, and the field it cannot reason about — a species ID joined to its cover estimate — is exactly the scientific judgment the project declines to infer (*no automated inference of scientific intent*) and the combination it must not make unseen (*do not silently discard a submitted edit*, read as: do not let a value reach the export that no author or coordinator stood behind). Restoring that safety by approving every merged record would erase the workload win, collapsing the option into a more complex way of reaching the same review queue. The maintenance-burden and inspectability criteria (both moderate, both resting on project tenets) point the same way. The team accepts the larger review queue deliberately.
+
+**What this forecloses.** The server will never combine field values across submissions. Visits whose only divergence is unrelated fields still require a coordinator's resolution; there is no automatic fast-path for them.
+
+**What this obligates downstream.**
+
+- *Tablet-sync* retains the base revision ID of the state each local draft started from and sends it in the submission envelope; it detects and shows the field worker when a visit it submitted is now unresolved on the server; its retry and idempotency handling is sized around the whole-payload envelope.
+- *Review-export* reads the resolved/unresolved status and the competing revisions to drive a revision-comparison review screen, records each resolution with the coordinator's identity and the contributing revision IDs, holds unresolved visits out of the Friday CSV, and draws exported values from current revisions only.
+
+Had field-aware merge been chosen instead, these obligations would have been a base-snapshot-plus-structured-changes envelope on the tablet and field-level provenance with an overlap-only review view on the export side. Recording the contrast here so a future reader weighing a reversal sees what the downstream nodes would have to become.
